# prefer.example — the repo .prefer executor-chain file, fully documented.
# Copy the lines you want to <repo>/.prefer (dotfiles-win: the nearest
# .prefer wins). Parsed by packages/suspenders/scripts/lib/exec-chain.ts
# (W183.2 chain + W813 semantics). One directive per line, '#' comments.
# Values are single tokens: quoted or spaced values belong to the W519
# ROUTING plane (scripts/lib/prefer-routing.ts), never the chain.
#
# ── W813 ordering law ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Later directives win per position: a directive on a later line outranks
# one above it, so the chain head = the LAST directive. Within a
# comma-list the left target is tried first. The attempt walk (dispatch
# retry) advances head-first: a dead lane re-dispatch tries the next
# entry, overrun parks on the last.
#
# ── must= — owner intent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Protected: never silently rerouted by the allow-list or quota health
# (W228: surfaced with a loud NOTE, never rerouted).
must=glm-5.3-flash
# Comma-list form (W813) — one line, several targets, left tried first;
# the line ranks as ONE directive in the position law:
# must=glm-5.3-flash,copilot
# A must may name a HUB label (W813): the lane rides that hub's gateway
# with the default executor (no model pin — the hub routes the model):
# must=nas
#
# ── prefer= — soft directive ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same chain grammar as must=. Because later lines win, a prefer= line
# BELOW a must= line is genuinely tried FIRST (the must becomes the
# fallback). The W228 protection rides whatever directive is picked.
# prefer=glm-flash2
#
# ── can= — the developer override (W813) ────────────────────────────────
# When <target> is doable on THIS machine the must= lines are INVALIDATED
# (one-shot — no must edit needed); unavailable, the line vanishes and
# the must stands unchanged. Doability probes, all sync:
#   executor → its binary resolves (SUSPENDERS_<NAME>_BIN or install paths)
#   hub      → label known in stack.json / hubs.json, not in quiet hours
#   model    → proven on this machine (cv tally) with no quota exhaustion
# A later can= line wins. Target = a single model or hub token.
# can=copilot
# can=nas
#
# ── hub= / hub-url= — whole-chain hub pinning ────────────────────────────
# hub=Label resolves to a real endpoint (hub-locate.ts: env override →
# hub-url candidates → stack.json → hubs.json → mDNS); unreachable hub
# degrades to local belt, loud, never silent. hub-url= adds repo-declared
# one-off candidates tried first.
# hub=desktop
# hub-url=https://hub.example.local:4101
